 "stimulus_html": "<p>“Sir: We have now the great satisfaction of acquainting you and the Congress that the treaties with France are at length completed and signed. The first is a treaty of amity and commerce, much on the plan of that projected in Congress; the other is a treaty of alliance, in which it is stipulated that in case England declares war against France, or occasions a war by attempts to hinder her commerce with us, we should then make common cause of it and join our forces and councils, etc. The great aim of this treaty is declared to be to ‘establish the liberty, sovereignty, and independency, absolute and unlimited, of the United States, as well in matters of government as commerce;’ and this is guarantied to us by France, together with all the countries we possess or shall possess at the conclusion of the war; in return for which the States guaranty to France all its possessions in America.”</p>\n<p align=\"right\">Benjamin Franklin, February 8, 1778</p>\n<p><i>Refer to the excerpt provided.</i></p>",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 614975,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Which of the following best describes the context of the excerpt provided?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1167872,
     "content_html": "The publication of the Declaration of Independence inspired foreign powers to support the nascent nation."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167873,
     "content_html": "The defeat of the British at Saratoga proved that the Americans had the strategic ability to win the war. United by their mutual resentment of the British, the Americans and the French agreed to work together until the colonies were free and not to make separate treaties with the British."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167871,
     "content_html": "Patrick Henry’s fiery rhetoric was printed in newspapers all over the world and articulated many reasons why the struggle for independence was worth supporting."
    },
    {
     "id": 1167870,
     "content_html": "The savage colonial defeat at Bunker Hill was so extreme, it caught the attention of the empathetic French."
    }
   ],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "multiple-choice"
   ],
   "combo_choices": []
  },
  {
   "id": 614976,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "What was the primary goal of the French in signing the treaty described in the except provided?",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1167877,
     "content_html": "Undermining their imperial rival, Great Britain"
    },
    {
     "id": 1167876,
     "content_html": "Supporting the cause of freedom and independence"
    },
    {
     "id": 1167875,
     "content_html": "Continuing their lucrative slave trade to the Americas"
    },
    {
     "id": 1167874,
     "content_html": "Becoming the first nation to recognize a former colony"
    }
   ],
